  TechniqueSection(
    title: "Why it matters",
    body:
        "Half-time keeps the tempo but puts one backbeat in the bar instead of two, on 3. The song suddenly feels twice as wide and heavy without anyone slowing down — the classic move for a big chorus or a slow, crushing riff.",
  ),
  TechniqueSection(
    title: "How to play it",
    body:
        "Hi-hat eighths with an accent on 1 and 3, the snare only on 3, hard and full. The kick plays 1 and the \"and\" of 2 and leaves the back half of the bar open so the snare can breathe.",
  ),
  TechniqueSection(
    title: "Practice tips",
    body:
        "The danger is rushing the snare on 3: let the space before it hang. Line 4 swaps the backbeat for a cross-stick, line 5 adds ghost notes in front of it, line 8 doubles the hi-hat to sixteenths over the same slow backbeat — big feel, busy surface.",
  ),
  TechniqueSection(
    title: "Where you hear it",
    body:
        "The Police \"Walking on the Moon\" (Stewart Copeland's backbeat on 3), the big half-time choruses of Foo Fighters, countless metal breakdowns and the slow, wide drops of modern pop.",
